    "reading": {
        "body": "Sweden's listed data center footprint is concentrated in the north, where Microsoft, Meta, and Kolo cluster around Luleå and the northern interior.\n\nThe pattern is latitude. The northern entries, Meta's three Luleå sites, Microsoft's eight, Kolo's Piteå, and RISE's ICE Datacenter, sit between roughly 60 and 66 degrees north. The southern entries are smaller and more dispersed: colocation and service facilities in Stockholm, Malmö, Lund, Göteborg, and Trelleborg. The named hyperscale operators are almost entirely in the north, while the south holds the smaller colocation and enterprise sites.\n\nThat split matters for AI because northern Sweden offers cheap hydroelectric power and cold air for cooling, the two inputs that dominate data center operating cost. Compute scales where power is cheap and cooling is free, and this list shows operators placing large facilities accordingly. The southern sites look like latency-driven colocation serving nearby population and industry rather than training-scale capacity.\n\nThe data does not give capacity, power draw, or status for any facility, so it cannot show how much compute actually sits at these locations. It also does not say whether the multiple Microsoft and Meta entries are distinct buildings or parts of single campuses. The count of 25 is a count of listed sites, not a measure of Swedish data center capacity.",
